  "response": "## Pick: `dateLabel` (src/date.ts:39–42)\n\n- **Receiver whose methods disagree:** `d` (the `Date` bound on line 40).\n- **Disagreement:** `d.getUTCFullYear()` (UTC family) mixed with `d.getMonth()` and `d.getDate()` (local family) — confirmed by `crime_00003` flagging `d` at L9/L41.\n- Why this function first over `todayKey`: `todayKey` is already self-consistent (all `getUTC*`). `dateLabel` is the actual mixed-family receiver and is the smaller, isolated fix.\n\n## Proposed refactor — commit to the UTC family\n\n`dateLabel` is a key-shaped string (`YYYY-MM-DD`), so UTC is the right family (stable across hosts, matches `todayKey`'s convention). While we're here, replace the hand-rolled concat with `toISOString().slice(0, 10)` so we also pick up zero-padding and the 0-indexed-month fix the comment flags.\n\n```ts\nexport function dateLabel(): string {\n  return new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10);\n}\n```\n\nThat single line eliminates three findings on this function: `mixed_utc_local_methods` (crime_00003), `date_string_concat` (crime_00024), and the `getMonth()` half of the receiver split. The remaining `new Date()` belongs to the broader `inject_clock` cleanup (crime_00002) — out of scope for this refactor.\n\nIf a display-locale label is ever wanted, do it at the boundary with `Intl.DateTimeFormat(locale, { timeZone: \"UTC\", … }).format(d)` rather than re-mixing families here.",
